WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.658 --> 00:00:02.929
- I'm competing with my job.

00:00:02.929 --> 00:00:05.745
Often on the other side
of what you're afraid of,

00:00:05.745 --> 00:00:07.772
that you experience really great things.

00:00:07.772 --> 00:00:10.439
It's so hard to say no to stuff.

00:00:11.775 --> 00:00:13.920
I think you always want
to be challenging yourself

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.121
and pushing yourself in
the work that you do,

00:00:16.121 --> 00:00:18.831
because that's how you
experience those situations.

00:00:18.831 --> 00:00:21.153
What's the impediment to
action advances action.

00:00:21.153 --> 00:00:22.798
What stands in the way becomes the way.

00:00:22.798 --> 00:00:24.457
I try to think about this in my own life.

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:26.535
- Hello, Believe Nation, it's Evan.

00:00:26.535 --> 00:00:28.021
My one word is believe

00:00:28.021 --> 00:00:31.138
and I believe in people more
than they believe in themselves

00:00:31.138 --> 00:00:34.187
and my sincere hope is
that if you see in yourself

00:00:34.187 --> 00:00:35.660
what I see in you,

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.658
you'll be able to change the planet.

00:00:37.658 --> 00:00:38.822
So to help you in your journey,

00:00:38.822 --> 00:00:40.586
today we're going to learn
from author, marketer,

00:00:40.586 --> 00:00:42.418
and entrepreneur Ryan Holiday

00:00:42.418 --> 00:00:46.251
and my take on his Top 10
Rules to Success, Volume II.

00:00:46.251 --> 00:00:48.038
Rule number eight is my personal favorite,

00:00:48.038 --> 00:00:50.868
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:50.868 --> 00:00:52.370
Also, as you're watching,
if you hear something

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:53.608
that really resonates with you,

00:00:53.608 --> 00:00:55.259
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:55.259 --> 00:00:56.695
and put quotes around
it so that other people

00:00:56.695 --> 00:00:58.507
can be inspired by it, as well.

00:00:58.507 --> 00:01:00.463
And if you leave it within
the first couple hours

00:01:00.463 --> 00:01:01.620
of this video going live,

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.347
you have a chance to win
one of two daily prizes.

00:01:04.347 --> 00:01:07.493
(whooshing)

00:01:07.493 --> 00:01:10.410
(empowering music)

00:01:17.628 --> 00:01:20.404
- I would rather challenge
myself in my career

00:01:20.404 --> 00:01:23.904
than challenge myself on a roller coaster,

00:01:25.765 --> 00:01:27.790
which is totally pointless.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:29.373
And I would rather,

00:01:30.249 --> 00:01:32.640
like for instance, I love running.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.388
I run almost every single day.

00:01:34.388 --> 00:01:35.688
But I don't do any races.

00:01:35.688 --> 00:01:39.525
I don't feel the need to
have artificial competition

00:01:39.525 --> 00:01:40.842
in my life.

00:01:40.842 --> 00:01:43.919
Like I'm competing with my job.

00:01:43.919 --> 00:01:44.762
Do you know what I'm,

00:01:44.762 --> 00:01:46.279
I'm trying to write the best,

00:01:46.279 --> 00:01:47.712
I'm trying to challenge
myself with my writing.

00:01:47.712 --> 00:01:51.648
Running for me is something that I enjoy.

00:01:51.648 --> 00:01:55.700
And to me the challenge is
like can I do it every day?

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:59.867
Not like did I beat 200 other
people who I don't care about?

00:02:00.917 --> 00:02:01.967
- Well, I think the point for,

00:02:01.967 --> 00:02:03.493
the one thing you've
added is I think sometimes

00:02:03.493 --> 00:02:06.142
we have these artificial
expectations that are unnecessary.

00:02:06.142 --> 00:02:08.873
Right, you're like, "I run
because I enjoy running.

00:02:08.873 --> 00:02:09.978
"I'm not trying to be competitive."

00:02:09.978 --> 00:02:11.931
And for your writing, you are competitive,

00:02:11.931 --> 00:02:13.154
but with yourself.

00:02:13.154 --> 00:02:15.373
- I think being present
and enjoying what happened

00:02:15.373 --> 00:02:16.456
is important.

00:02:17.896 --> 00:02:20.432
But I guess what I'm saying
is like yesterday I ran.

00:02:20.432 --> 00:02:21.941
I did an hour.

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:24.518
And it was, you know,
92, 93 degrees outside.

00:02:24.518 --> 00:02:26.018
It was hot, right?

00:02:27.673 --> 00:02:28.706
But I didn't say, like,

00:02:28.706 --> 00:02:31.833
is this going to be enjoyable or not?

00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:34.048
I said look, this is what do.

00:02:34.048 --> 00:02:36.711
And come hell or high
water, I'm going to do it.

00:02:36.711 --> 00:02:38.711
And it was enjoy, I got,

00:02:40.063 --> 00:02:41.727
it wasn't enjoyable at first.

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:43.737
But I pushed through.

00:02:43.737 --> 00:02:46.238
It's like on the other
side of that resistance

00:02:46.238 --> 00:02:48.133
is often that thing.

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:50.186
And so I think that's
the problem with people

00:02:50.186 --> 00:02:52.969
who are like, you know, "I'll
just sort of go with the flow.

00:02:52.969 --> 00:02:54.105
"I'll take it as it happens."

00:02:54.105 --> 00:02:56.916
That can work, but, you know,

00:02:56.916 --> 00:03:00.088
it's often on the other side
of what you're afraid of

00:03:00.088 --> 00:03:02.091
that you experience really great things.

00:03:02.091 --> 00:03:04.523
And you have to have the
power to push through that.

00:03:04.523 --> 00:03:07.413
What do you feel the most satisfied doing?

00:03:07.413 --> 00:03:09.913
And what do you feel the
most sort of fulfilled

00:03:09.913 --> 00:03:11.413
or creative doing?

00:03:12.729 --> 00:03:15.271
And then that's going
to be the sort of gift

00:03:15.271 --> 00:03:17.550
that you protect at all times.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:20.566
And then you've got to
find the other things

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:21.966
that you're either good at,

00:03:21.966 --> 00:03:23.484
but you don't want to be doing,

00:03:23.484 --> 00:03:26.283
or you're really bad at,
that take a lot of your time.

00:03:26.283 --> 00:03:30.024
And you've got to be able to
delegate and find services

00:03:30.024 --> 00:03:33.093
or companies or other people

00:03:33.093 --> 00:03:36.044
that you can hire to help
you with those things.

00:03:36.044 --> 00:03:37.880
And like that's really,

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:39.481
that's always been something
that I've struggled with

00:03:39.481 --> 00:03:41.927
that I think is a sort
of perennial struggle

00:03:41.927 --> 00:03:44.745
of any kind of leadership
or entrepreneurship.

00:03:44.745 --> 00:03:45.959
How do you,

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:49.914
how do you build the
organization or the structure

00:03:49.914 --> 00:03:54.059
that you need so you can continue
to do what you need to do

00:03:54.059 --> 00:03:58.348
as the boss or as the main creative force?

00:03:58.348 --> 00:04:01.556
And you've got to be able
to continually evaluate

00:04:01.556 --> 00:04:04.275
your own performance in
that sense and say like,

00:04:04.275 --> 00:04:06.851
look, this is what I'm really good at.

00:04:06.851 --> 00:04:08.712
This is what I'm not good at.

00:04:08.712 --> 00:04:11.172
Here's someone who's better at it than me.

00:04:11.172 --> 00:04:14.821
I'm considering it an
investment to hire that person

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:17.240
or to bring on that service provider.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:20.855
And I'm doing is so that I
can continue to be as good

00:04:20.855 --> 00:04:25.009
as I need to be at my
like sort of core thing.

00:04:25.009 --> 00:04:26.851
It's 8 a.m., like I got
to get to the office.

00:04:26.851 --> 00:04:28.404
And I was like why?

00:04:28.404 --> 00:04:29.548
Why do I have to get?

00:04:29.548 --> 00:04:32.664
I always wanted to be a writer.

00:04:32.664 --> 00:04:34.574
I accomplished this thing.

00:04:34.574 --> 00:04:36.789
I can make a living being a writer.

00:04:36.789 --> 00:04:37.792
I have a home.

00:04:37.792 --> 00:04:40.373
Like, I haven't been there in weeks.

00:04:40.373 --> 00:04:42.668
Like, why am I here?

00:04:42.668 --> 00:04:46.358
And I was like, I was here
because like I just said yes

00:04:46.358 --> 00:04:48.441
and I didn't think about,

00:04:49.373 --> 00:04:53.540
I didn't actually weigh the
pros and cons of this scenario.

00:04:54.428 --> 00:04:56.103
And again, it's like
something you got to do

00:04:56.103 --> 00:04:58.164
what you don't want to do to
figure out what you want to do.

00:04:58.164 --> 00:04:59.497
But it was like,

00:05:01.067 --> 00:05:03.779
I'd sort distanced myself
from a lot of things.

00:05:03.779 --> 00:05:06.222
And I'd gotten my life in a good place.

00:05:06.222 --> 00:05:09.045
And then someone called me and
offered me money on the phone

00:05:09.045 --> 00:05:10.170
and I said yes.

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:12.747
Because it's so hard to say,

00:05:12.747 --> 00:05:14.497
like it's so hard to,

00:05:16.232 --> 00:05:18.824
to say no to stuff, especially
if you're like a doer.

00:05:18.824 --> 00:05:21.772
It's so hard to say
like, I want to do this,

00:05:21.772 --> 00:05:23.439
but I also want to do that.

00:05:23.439 --> 00:05:24.515
I have to choose.

00:05:24.515 --> 00:05:26.726
It's easy to say like, I'm
going to try to do both

00:05:26.726 --> 00:05:27.845
at the same time.

00:05:27.845 --> 00:05:29.762
And I said yes to both.

00:05:30.714 --> 00:05:34.814
Like, I don't think I've
ever like quit anything.

00:05:34.814 --> 00:05:36.707
Like I've just added new stuff.

00:05:36.707 --> 00:05:39.830
And so for me that certainly
has added up over time

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:42.498
to an unsustainable load.

00:05:42.498 --> 00:05:44.250
We all wake up the same.

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:45.807
Every day we both wake up

00:05:45.807 --> 00:05:48.813
and things don't go the way we're,

00:05:48.813 --> 00:05:50.776
like there's not just the
adversity when you're younger,

00:05:50.776 --> 00:05:52.738
by there's also the day-to-day realities.

00:05:52.738 --> 00:05:54.806
Like you run a business
and I run a business.

00:05:54.806 --> 00:05:56.473
And, you know, this,

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:01.159
you have a problem with
your accounting software.

00:06:01.159 --> 00:06:04.169
And then you're having problems
with Skype this morning.

00:06:04.169 --> 00:06:07.169
There's also those minor situations.

00:06:08.031 --> 00:06:10.700
And how you choose to
deal with them determines

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.674
whether you're going to have
a good day or a bad day,

00:06:13.674 --> 00:06:16.253
just in the same way that
other situations determine

00:06:16.253 --> 00:06:18.515
whether you're going to have
a good life or a bad life.

00:06:18.515 --> 00:06:21.044
And how you choose to respond,

00:06:21.044 --> 00:06:22.369
like if you,

00:06:22.369 --> 00:06:24.139
you know, Skype wasn't working.

00:06:24.139 --> 00:06:26.386
And if you said like, if you got mad,

00:06:26.386 --> 00:06:28.910
if you decided this was horrible,

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:30.710
if you got really frustrated, it might,

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:33.111
not only would that
mess with your attitude,

00:06:33.111 --> 00:06:33.944
but then,

00:06:35.313 --> 00:06:37.302
it doesn't help you with the situation

00:06:37.302 --> 00:06:39.841
that you then have to
deal with which is okay,

00:06:39.841 --> 00:06:41.225
how am I going to find a solution

00:06:41.225 --> 00:06:42.608
and how am I going to do this interview

00:06:42.608 --> 00:06:44.206
and how am I going to
get the best out of it?

00:06:44.206 --> 00:06:45.661
Like I'm giving a talk.

00:06:45.661 --> 00:06:47.604
Let's say I'm giving a talk
in front of an audience.

00:06:47.604 --> 00:06:49.234
I don't control the audience.

00:06:49.234 --> 00:06:51.325
I don't control, you know,

00:06:51.325 --> 00:06:53.732
the weather, I don't
control any of these things.

00:06:53.732 --> 00:06:56.718
But I do control what I
bring to that situation.

00:06:56.718 --> 00:06:59.218
So I want to focus the
entirety of my energy on doing

00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:02.906
the best possible job and
salvaging the most out of it.

00:07:02.906 --> 00:07:05.385
I think that the big problem
that many people have

00:07:05.385 --> 00:07:07.738
is they don't know
what's important to them.

00:07:07.738 --> 00:07:11.402
And so they end up getting
distracted by things

00:07:11.402 --> 00:07:14.319
that other people are doing
or things that other people

00:07:14.319 --> 00:07:16.892
are trying to do to them.

00:07:16.892 --> 00:07:20.160
And so, like we were talking
about with my business,

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.097
early on, I'm thinking
okay, this is the way

00:07:23.097 --> 00:07:24.528
you're supposed to do things,

00:07:24.528 --> 00:07:26.694
because this is how everyone
else runs their business.

00:07:26.694 --> 00:07:28.538
What I didn't stop to do was think

00:07:28.538 --> 00:07:30.626
what kind of business do I want

00:07:30.626 --> 00:07:33.343
and what kind of life am I trying to have?

00:07:33.343 --> 00:07:35.833
And when I was actually able
to have that conversation,

00:07:35.833 --> 00:07:39.364
I was able to cut out a
bunch of extraneous things.

00:07:39.364 --> 00:07:41.985
It makes the most sense
for my personal happiness,

00:07:41.985 --> 00:07:45.557
and my personal success to
try to make as much money

00:07:45.557 --> 00:07:49.136
as possible for as least
amount of time commitment

00:07:49.136 --> 00:07:52.328
as possible, so I can
write as much as possible,

00:07:52.328 --> 00:07:56.527
versus trying to make as
much money as possible,

00:07:56.527 --> 00:07:59.171
however possible, right?

00:07:59.171 --> 00:08:03.293
And so, sometimes you've got
to do the wrong thing to,

00:08:03.293 --> 00:08:04.713
like you got to touch
the stove and go like,

00:08:04.713 --> 00:08:06.167
this is really hot.

00:08:06.167 --> 00:08:08.032
I don't want to do this ever again.

00:08:08.032 --> 00:08:10.610
And so I sort of pivoted my business.

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:13.460
And they say like it's not a principal

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:14.467
till it costs you money.

00:08:14.467 --> 00:08:17.053
Like one of the other partners went off,

00:08:17.053 --> 00:08:19.015
started a different company and has made,

00:08:19.015 --> 00:08:20.754
you know, has done very well for himself.

00:08:20.754 --> 00:08:24.380
And we've passed on a lot of projects

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:26.412
that went on to do pretty well.

00:08:26.412 --> 00:08:28.533
So like, you see,

00:08:28.533 --> 00:08:30.482
like, there's a cost.

00:08:30.482 --> 00:08:33.269
So like having the life
you want, there's a cost.

00:08:33.269 --> 00:08:35.369
And you got to be ready to pay that cost.

00:08:35.369 --> 00:08:38.941
And the worst thing you
can do is try to have like,

00:08:38.941 --> 00:08:41.231
try to have your cake and eat it, too,

00:08:41.231 --> 00:08:43.349
like try to have the life you want

00:08:43.349 --> 00:08:44.747
while doing the things you don't,

00:08:44.747 --> 00:08:46.180
it just doesn't work.

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:48.221
I think you always want
to be challenging yourself

00:08:48.221 --> 00:08:50.546
and pushing yourself in
the work that you do,

00:08:50.546 --> 00:08:53.219
because that's how you
experience those situations.

00:08:53.219 --> 00:08:55.623
You know, it's very easy once
you get to a certain point

00:08:55.623 --> 00:08:58.796
to be, to sort of find your
comfort zone and stay in it

00:08:58.796 --> 00:09:00.112
and not be challenged.

00:09:00.112 --> 00:09:01.669
But that's not to say
that life won't visit

00:09:01.669 --> 00:09:03.265
challenges upon you.

00:09:03.265 --> 00:09:05.273
But you're going to
experience less, right?

00:09:05.273 --> 00:09:08.886
But I think exercise and
working out is a great metaphor

00:09:08.886 --> 00:09:11.946
for this, like I try to
run every single day.

00:09:11.946 --> 00:09:14.132
And I don't care what the weather is.

00:09:14.132 --> 00:09:16.753
I don't care how I'm feeling.

00:09:16.753 --> 00:09:19.133
It's something that I'm
going to subject myself to

00:09:19.133 --> 00:09:20.439
and force myself to.

00:09:20.439 --> 00:09:22.399
And you know, often I'll find like,

00:09:22.399 --> 00:09:24.479
I run in the afternoons, for instance.

00:09:24.479 --> 00:09:25.999
And I'll find that I'll be tired,

00:09:25.999 --> 00:09:28.912
that the day won't have gone very well.

00:09:28.912 --> 00:09:31.707
That maybe I wasn't feeling great.

00:09:31.707 --> 00:09:34.934
But that hour that I'm out there sweating,

00:09:34.934 --> 00:09:37.925
when I come back, I'm ready to go again.

00:09:37.925 --> 00:09:39.395
I've been recharged.

00:09:39.395 --> 00:09:41.895
And I think being able to find

00:09:43.449 --> 00:09:46.317
that hey, I do better when I'm challenged

00:09:46.317 --> 00:09:48.786
is a great thing to teach yourself.

00:09:48.786 --> 00:09:52.172
And the other thing,
Tim Ferris told me this,

00:09:52.172 --> 00:09:55.961
he was saying, you know, if
you work out every single day,

00:09:55.961 --> 00:09:58.923
you have a guaranteed
win every single day.

00:09:58.923 --> 00:10:01.030
So your company might
be declaring bankruptcy,

00:10:01.030 --> 00:10:02.780
but you set a new PR,

00:10:04.011 --> 00:10:07.978
Or you got a great rush of endorphins,

00:10:07.978 --> 00:10:09.890
you'll at least be able
to tell yourself like,

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:11.328
hey, that went well.

00:10:11.328 --> 00:10:13.267
And that's something that I,

00:10:13.267 --> 00:10:15.742
that the reason I seek out
that hardship every day

00:10:15.742 --> 00:10:16.679
is that it's a hardship

00:10:16.679 --> 00:10:19.788
that I'm sort of guaranteed to overcome.

00:10:19.788 --> 00:10:21.643
As a creative,

00:10:21.643 --> 00:10:23.659
what the critics think of your work,

00:10:23.659 --> 00:10:25.686
what the audience thinks of your work,

00:10:25.686 --> 00:10:28.842
what gatekeepers or lists, or,

00:10:28.842 --> 00:10:32.139
you know, curators think of your work,

00:10:32.139 --> 00:10:34.423
these are the things that
are outside of your control.

00:10:34.423 --> 00:10:36.068
And you have to remind yourself of that.

00:10:36.068 --> 00:10:39.319
And as a creative, if you
go into what you're doing

00:10:39.319 --> 00:10:40.872
and you just say I'm
only going to be happy

00:10:40.872 --> 00:10:42.942
if this external thing happens, right,

00:10:42.942 --> 00:10:44.892
I'm only going to be
happy if it's published

00:10:44.892 --> 00:10:46.004
by this publisher.

00:10:46.004 --> 00:10:48.180
I'm only going to be happy
if it wins this award.

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:50.602
It's only going to make me happy
if make this amount of money.

00:10:50.602 --> 00:10:52.189
These are recipes for misery,

00:10:52.189 --> 00:10:53.735
because you've now put your happiness

00:10:53.735 --> 00:10:55.796
in the hands of someone else,

00:10:55.796 --> 00:10:57.919
someone who is indifferent to you,

00:10:57.919 --> 00:10:59.997
someone you don't control.

00:10:59.997 --> 00:11:02.111
You make a business decision,

00:11:02.111 --> 00:11:03.180
I've done these myself.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:05.606
You make a business decision or a choice

00:11:05.606 --> 00:11:07.426
and it turns out to be a mistake.

00:11:07.426 --> 00:11:08.551
You were totally wrong.

00:11:08.551 --> 00:11:10.810
You can see that as a mistake

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:12.240
and you can get down on yourself,

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:15.327
or you can see it as a
chance to get comfortable

00:11:15.327 --> 00:11:17.499
with failure, because
failure is a part of life.

00:11:17.499 --> 00:11:20.104
Where a scientist doesn't get upset

00:11:20.104 --> 00:11:22.676
when a hypothesis that they
had turns out to be wrong,

00:11:22.676 --> 00:11:23.956
because that's what they do.

00:11:23.956 --> 00:11:25.091
That's the whole point.

00:11:25.091 --> 00:11:27.976
They're learning each time
they do something wrong.

00:11:27.976 --> 00:11:30.957
Or a computer, I'm sure
as college students,

00:11:30.957 --> 00:11:32.298
a lot of you get this, as well.

00:11:32.298 --> 00:11:34.923
You spend weeks and
weeks working on a paper

00:11:34.923 --> 00:11:36.753
and the computer deletes it.

00:11:36.753 --> 00:11:38.719
A glitch erases the work that you've had.

00:11:38.719 --> 00:11:40.469
So you can get upset.

00:11:41.614 --> 00:11:42.885
You can deal with it.

00:11:42.885 --> 00:11:44.309
What I did the last
time this happened to me

00:11:44.309 --> 00:11:46.597
was you get so upset
that you kick a trash can

00:11:46.597 --> 00:11:50.593
across the room and it makes
a huge dent in the wall.

00:11:50.593 --> 00:11:54.574
Or you can see this as
a chance to start over.

00:11:54.574 --> 00:11:56.385
You can see it as a chance to,

00:11:56.385 --> 00:11:58.586
now that you've been through
it once, to do it again,

00:11:58.586 --> 00:12:01.311
and ultimately produce a piece of work

00:12:01.311 --> 00:12:02.988
that's of higher quality

00:12:02.988 --> 00:12:06.116
than maybe you originally
intended to turn in.

00:12:06.116 --> 00:12:08.305
You can use that as a chance, actually,

00:12:08.305 --> 00:12:11.416
to raise your standard, rather
than simply muddle through.

00:12:11.416 --> 00:12:14.006
What's the impediment to
action advances action.

00:12:14.006 --> 00:12:15.530
What stands in the way becomes the way.

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:17.491
I try to think about this in my own life,

00:12:17.491 --> 00:12:20.345
whenever I'm facing any kind of situation.

00:12:20.345 --> 00:12:22.590
And I've yet to find one
that isn't made better

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:24.818
from this attitude, that isn't made better

00:12:24.818 --> 00:12:27.688
by seeing each and every
obstacle as a chance

00:12:27.688 --> 00:12:29.783
to practice excellence in its,

00:12:29.783 --> 00:12:30.956
in a new form.

00:12:30.956 --> 00:12:34.405
And you know, I think today,
we all face our own problems.

00:12:34.405 --> 00:12:35.643
There's tough economic times.

00:12:35.643 --> 00:12:38.174
We have a political situation that's uh.

00:12:38.174 --> 00:12:42.536
We all face individual
obstacles as a daily basis

00:12:42.536 --> 00:12:44.632
and we culturally face obstacles.

00:12:44.632 --> 00:12:48.804
But we have a chance to see
those things like they saw them,

00:12:48.804 --> 00:12:52.067
which is to not only not see them as bad,

00:12:52.067 --> 00:12:54.190
but to see them as opportunities,

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:57.803
as chances to improve,
or to practice something

00:12:57.803 --> 00:13:01.970
that perhaps we wouldn't
have otherwise practiced.

